WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
five million  eight hundred and fifty-one it did not  come as a surprise  president johary museveni has maintained  a decisive lead  against his opponents from the start of  the vote count  the electoral commission declares yahweh  russell  tibu kaguta elected president of the  republic of uganda  the president addressed the nation after  his victory was declared  and say despite attempts by some  individuals to tamper with the biometric  identification kits  the election was largely free and fair i  congratulate you  for turning up in big numbers  and voting for the candidates  and for the parties of your choice i  think  this may turn out to be the most  cheating free  election since 1962  supporters of the 76 year old incumbent  turned out to celebrate  every gander must support his excellence  recently he's the one who knows us  the electoral commissioners say they've  done their best under the circumstances  but already  the results have been rejected by bobby  wein who has said  he has evidence of widespread illegal  irregularities including  rigging in favor of president johari  museveni  his team members say they are  considering  moving to court to challenge the results  there has also been  other forms of rigging that are not  restricted  to fortification of declaration forms  including chasing away of agents at the  polling station  including forcing people to vote in the  open  yet under the lawyers supposed to have a  sacred barrel  the results saw wine received nearly 35  of the vote and his party the national  unity platform  has garnered a sizable number of  legislative seats  however he told al jazeera that his  movements have been restricted  and visitors including journalists have  been blocked from accessing his compound  by soldiers surrounding his home  security officials say  they had information that he was  planning to declare himself winner  there's also been heavy deployment of  police and the military in the capital  and other urban areas perceived to be  opposition strongholds  the winner may have been declared but  the country  remains on edge catherine sawyer  al-jazeera  kampala

